Jaffna, Mannar, Vavuniya, Batticaloa, Trincomalee and Mullaitivu were wins for the TULF, despite them and the UNP winning the same number of seats in Mannar, Trincomalee and Vavuniya, the TULF had the majority of votes and thus the Chairmanship of those councils.

Due to our boycott all other districts were won by the UNP, including Kandy, Nuwara Eliya, Matara, Kurunegala, Moneragala, Ratnapura, and Kegalle where the UNP ran completely uncontested. Every other district is a victory for the UNP with a few seats won by the JVP, who despite their high hopes did not win any district outright.

It was unlikely we would have won much if we took part, at the very least the legitimacy of the elections has been called into question. Voter turnout was low and there are alleged reports of inconsistencies with several counting stations receiving their ballot boxes late. Rumours are that as much as 10% of casted ballots are spoiled. 

In the days before voting as part of what the police said were measures to ensure the elections went smoothly, several TULF leaders & MP's including Amirthalingam, VN Navaratnam, V Dharmalingham and M Sivasithambaram were arrested and detained briefly and their offices searched.

The situation combined with the violence that had taken place just before the voting was enough to rile up Election Commissioner Piyasekera, enough to where he directly berrated the president himself and threatened to resign.

Whether its all an indication of discontent with the UNP regime is too be seen.

- @return: Our relationships with the other boycotting parties have improved.
